course: dictionary
lesson: 10928
title: Vote: a word that works two ways
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Vote can be a doing word or a naming word. As a doing word, it means to choose by raising a hand, ticking a paper or pressing a button, so that a group can decide. «The class will vote on the name for our new reading corner.» The children are choosing, so vote is a doing word here.
explain: As a naming word, a vote is one person's choice in a decision, or the act of choosing as a group. «Let's have a vote.» The words around it tell you that the group is about to decide something.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in choose and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. «The class will choose on the name for our new reading corner» does not sound right, so look for the other clues: a sentence about a group deciding is a clue to a verb, and words like «let's have a» point to a naming word.
fact: Vote as a verb: to vote, votes, voted, voting.
fact: Vote as a noun: a vote, votes.
fact: «Vote» means to choose by hand, ticket or button, so that a group can decide.
fact: «The most votes wins» means the choice with the most votes is the winner.
fact: In the UK you do not vote for the prime minister directly.
exercise: «The Grammy Awards are voted on by members of the Recording Academy, people who work in music, not by fans.» Who chooses the Grammy Awards? | The prime minister | Fans of the singers | Only children | *Members of the Recording Academy, people who work in music
exercise: Maya says, «Let's have a vote on the class trip.» What is a vote here? | *A choice made by a group | Choosing a new teacher alone | Something you eat | A kind of game
exercise: «They argue about ideas and vote on new laws.» What job does vote do in this sentence? | A feeling of sadness | A naming word for a place | The opposite of argue | *A doing word, to choose
test: «Jordan got three votes for captain.» In this sentence, what is votes? | *A naming word: three people's choices | A place to sit | A doing word: to jump | A kind of drink
test: What does «the most votes wins» mean? | The vote is cancelled | *The choice with the most votes wins | The person who shouts loudest wins | Everyone wins the same prize
exam: Which sentence uses vote as a naming word? | «She will vote tomorrow.» | *«Let's have a vote on the picnic food.» | «We vote for the best idea.» | «They vote on new laws.»
